          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Edith Wharton</strong><br>\nEdith Wharton was born into a wealthy New York family in 1862. As a child, she traveled extensively in Europe with her parents, which influenced her writing later in life. Wharton had private tutors and accessed vast libraries, which allowed her to learn multiple languages and read widely. Despite the limited formal education opportunities for women at the time, her family's wealth and status provided her with an excellent intellectual environment.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Beginning of Literary Career</strong><br>\nWharton began writing poems and stories in her youth, but her first major publication came later, in 1897, with the book \"The Decoration of Houses,\" co-written with architect Ogden Codman. In 1902, she published her first novel, \"The Valley of Decision,\" which was followed by many more. Her early literary work reflected her knowledge of the inner workings of upper-class society.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence and Themes</strong><br>\nThe works of Edith Wharton often explored the rigid social customs and expectations of her time. She focused on the limitations faced by women in the late 19th and early 20th centuries. Her narratives were marked by keen observations on social class and the role of women, which was groundbreaking for her era.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
