          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>\"The House of Mirth\"</strong><br>\nThe House of Mirth, published in 1905, was one of Wharton's first critical and commercial successes. The novel follows the tragic life of Lily Bart, a woman who is both a victim of and participant in the superficial society that ultimately leads to her downfall. This book secured Wharton's reputation as a major novelist.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Pulitzer Prize for \"The Age of Innocence\"</strong><br>\nIn 1921, Edith Wharton became the first woman to win the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction for her novel \"The Age of Innocence.\" The book scrutinizes the contradictions within the upper-class society of New York City during the 1870s, and it is often praised for its insight and detailed descriptions.</p>\n\n<p><strong>World War I Efforts</strong><br>\nDuring World War I, Wharton was living in France and dedicated herself to humanitarian efforts. She worked in charitable endeavors for refugees, was involved in fundraising, and even reported from the front lines. Her work during the war was recognized by the French government, which awarded her the Legion of Honor.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
