          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Wharton's Later Works</strong><br>\nIn her later years, Wharton's writing continued to be prolific. She published many novels, short stories, and non-fiction works. Among her later notable works is \"The Writing of Fiction,\" which is a treatise on the art of writing. Wharton continued to write until her death in 1937, leaving behind a rich legacy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Relationships</strong><br>\nWharton's personal life was complex and often unhappy. She married Edward Robbins Wharton, but the marriage was troubled and ended in divorce. Her experiences and observations of the societal elite infused her writing with realism and depth, drawing from her own life as well as the lives around her.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Edith Wharton's Legacy</strong><br>\nEdith Wharton's legacy as a writer is significant. She paved the way for future American women writers, with her sophisticated portrayals of social life and her success in a literary landscape dominated by men. Her home, The Mount, in Massachusetts, is now a museum that honors her life and works. Her contributions to literature continue to be studied and appreciated today.</p>\n"
              },
